---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Evidence from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import dgs from "../data/dgs-audit.json";
import traps from "../data/forename-traps.json";
import zeros from "../data/false-zeros.json";
---
<Base title="Method"
      eyebrow="How this archive decides what it will say"
      dek="The same method as the Falco and Defranceski archives. Every claim is tiered, and the tier is shown. An archive that cannot say what it does not know is not an archive.">

  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The tiers</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></td>
          <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan itself.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip">Inferred</span></td>
          <td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so that it can be
            overturned by anyone who finds it wanting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip">Superseded</span></td>
          <td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that says otherwise. The old
            claim is kept visible, not deleted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip inf">Disputed</span></td>
          <td>Asserted in the family record but unsupported, or contradicted, by what can be seen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip lore">Family lore</span></td>
          <td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because pretending
            otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>This archive starts at the bottom of the ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly everything specific to this family is presently <strong>family lore</strong>. The Argentine
    origin, Rosario, the consulship, the horses, the ostriches — all of it is testimony, and it is presented
    as testimony. What is <strong>documented</strong> so far is largely <em>context</em>: the rarity and
    distribution of the surname, the town of Llerena, the Argentine Stud Book, the Oudtshoorn feather boom.
  </p>
  <p>
    Context is not evidence about a family. It is the backdrop against which that family has to be placed,
    and it is worth having early because it tells you which claims are likely, which are hard, and where to
    look. It is never allowed to stand in for a record.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Rarity is evidence, and it has limits</h2>
  <p>
    With 2,124 bearers worldwide, <em>Lerena</em> is rare enough that clustering carries real information —
    the 61 in South Africa are very unlikely to be unrelated strangers. The archive will therefore let
    distribution <strong>propose</strong> connections.
  </p>
  <p>
    It will not let distribution <strong>prove</strong> them. A proposal built on rarity is written down as
    inferred, with its reasoning exposed, and it stays inferred until a register says otherwise.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Living people</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Living people are omitted from the build entirely</strong> — not hidden, not gated, not present
    in the output. Because this line reaches the present within three generations, that removes a
    substantial part of the story from public view, including most of the South African and all of the
    Australian chapter. The material is kept in the private working files; it is simply not published.
  </p>
  <p>
    Public figures are treated differently, and only as public figures: a jockey's race record or a
    breeder's stud book entry is published information and may be cited as such. No claim of relationship to
    a living person is made from a shared surname.
  </p>
  <p>
    A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>A null is not a finding until a control says so</h2>
  <p class="dek">Most of what this archive has established is negative &mdash; that Pablo Armando is
    not in the Rosario baptisms, that Juan Carlos Lerena is in no record anywhere. Negative findings
    are only as good as the search that produced them, and a search can fail in a way that looks
    exactly like an answer.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>On 14 September 2026 this archive nearly recorded three false nulls in ten minutes.</strong>
      FamilySearch&rsquo;s results page renders the string
      <em>&ldquo;Historical Record Search Results (0)&rdquo;</em> <strong>while the query is still
      running</strong> &mdash; a complete, composed, confident page that cannot be told apart from a
      genuinely empty result.</p>
    <p><strong>It was caught only by running a control.</strong> The same query shape was aimed at
      <em>Sancho Lerena</em>, a man for whom five records had been pulled twenty minutes earlier. It
      returned zero too. Since <em>that</em> answer was known to be false, the fault had to be in the
      query rather than in the name. Waiting longer returned the five records &mdash; and then 210, and
      then 822.</p>
    <p>This is the same class of failure as the zero-length response body already logged against this
      source: <strong>the service failing in a way that resembles success</strong>. It is worse than an
      outage, because an outage announces itself.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So the rule: a null is not written down until a control query, in the same
      form, in the same session, returns a result already known to exist.</strong> The archive learned
      this once before and more expensively, over the 1895 census, where it read an enumerator&rsquo;s
      abbreviation as a husband&rsquo;s surname with no control case and had to retract within the hour.
      The second time the lesson arrived it cost nothing.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">15 September 2026 &mdash; the rule decided four questions in
      one day, and only one of them the way it was expected to</p>
    <p><strong>It saved a day of work once.</strong> This archive had planned to read three hundred
      images of a regiment&rsquo;s chaplaincy book by hand, looking for Sancho Llerena. Before starting,
      one control &mdash; a search for the chaplain&rsquo;s own name, <em>Berroeta</em> &mdash; showed the
      book <em>was</em> in the full-text index. His absence from it was therefore already evidence, and
      the three hundred images were never read.</p>
    <p><strong>It voided a null that looked clean.</strong> Searching Spanish records for
      <em>Félix Llerena and Rosalía Aguilar</em> returned zero. The control &mdash; asking what Andalusian
      collections exist at all &mdash; showed the full-text corpus for Andalusia holds
      <strong>nobility and notarial records, not parish registers</strong>. The zero meant
      <em>not findable by this instrument</em>, not <em>not there</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>It voided two more, in the same hour.</strong> Two exact-surname searches returned zero
      <em>with</em> the &ldquo;No Results Found&rdquo; line present &mdash; normally this archive&rsquo;s
      proof that a search ran. The control showed the same query shape <strong>could not retrieve a
      record known to be in the collection</strong>, because the indexed surname was a compound
      (<em>Lerena y Armenta</em>) that exact matching will not reach. <strong>A null from a query that
      cannot find what is there is not a null.</strong></p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>And once it confirmed a negative worth keeping.</strong> Sancho&rsquo;s burial
      is in no index. The control &mdash; a common surname, same city, same decade &mdash; returned church
      burials from <em>his own family&rsquo;s parish</em> dated 1832, 1833 and 1838. So the records exist,
      are indexed, and do not contain him. <strong>That is the only one of the four that survived, and it
      is the only one this archive would publish.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-false-zeros">Every disguise a false zero has worn</h2>
  <p class="dek">The rule above was not arrived at once. It was arrived at twelve times, and this is
    the catalogue &mdash; because a search that fails in a way that looks like an answer is the most
    dangerous thing this archive meets, and the next one will not look like any of these either.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Nine of these were written in working logs where no reader could see them</strong>
      &mdash; found on 20 September 2026 by asking which findings in this archive&rsquo;s 136 working
      files reach no page at all. The two dated that day were caught by the habit rather than by luck,
      which is the only argument for keeping a habit.</p>
    <p class="cap">On 15 September the working log recorded that the same trap had been seen
      <strong>four times in a month, in four different disguises</strong>. It is now twelve.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:14rem">The disguise</th><th style="width:16rem">What it looked like</th>
        <th style="width:16rem">What it actually was</th><th>How it was caught</th>
        <th class="m" style="width:6rem">When</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {zeros.map((r) => {
          const c = Object.values(r);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
              <td class="m">{c[4]}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>What they have in common is not the mechanism.</strong>
    A quota, a logout, a throttle, a block, a mislabelled reel and a counter that ignores its own
    parameters have nothing to do with one another. What they share is that
    <strong>each produced a page a careful person would have believed</strong>, and that in every single
    case the thing that caught it was a control query whose answer was already known.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:24px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And the mirror &mdash; a false <em>positive</em> that would
      have been believed</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A full-text search for the exact phrase <em>&ldquo;Sancho Llerena&rdquo;</em> returns four pages.
        Page one is this family. <strong>Pages two to four are Spanish nobility proofs, notarial books and
        military records &mdash; and not one of them contains a man of that name.</strong></p>
      <p>One was opened to find out why. A 1599 proof of nobility for the Order of Alc&aacute;ntara reads,
        in transcript: <em>&ldquo;&hellip;es familiar del <strong>SANCHO, OFICIO LLERENA</strong>&rdquo;</em>.
        It is <strong><em>Santo Oficio &hellip; Llerena</em></strong> &mdash; the Inquisition tribunal seated
        at Llerena in Badajoz. <strong>The machine reads <em>Santo Oficio</em> as <em>Sancho, oficio</em>,
        and the phrase search then matches <em>Sancho &hellip; Llerena</em> across the two words.</strong></p>
      <p>The Inquisition sat at Llerena for three centuries, so those words stand next to each other in
        thousands of Spanish documents. <strong>Any search for this family in Spain will drown in
        them</strong> &mdash; and this archive was one step from recording that <em>Sancho</em> is an
        attested forename in Spanish Llerena families. It is not. That false fact would then have been
        used, quietly, to argue against <strong>Francisco</strong>.</p>
      <p class="cap">A false zero is caught by a control. <strong>A false positive is caught only by
        opening the document</strong> &mdash; which is the other half of the same rule, and the more
        expensive half, because a hit that agrees with you asks to be believed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Before you list what to buy, list what you have</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <strong>16 September 2026</strong> this archive drew up a ranked list of files for a
      researcher going to the National Archives in Pretoria, where the first file costs
      <strong>R2,850</strong>. It put two Transvaal death notices first and second.</p>
    <p><strong>Both had been sitting on David&rsquo;s own disk, photographed, since November
      2021.</strong> The same folder held the 1911 marriage register, which was on the work list as a
      thing to <em>go and find</em>. Nobody had ever opened it.</p>
    <p>The failure was not carelessness about sources. It was a gap in the bookkeeping. This archive
      kept a careful <a href={u("/searched/")}>register of what it had read</a> and no register at all
      of <strong>what it already held</strong> &mdash; and the two are not the same thing. A document
      can be in the house, scanned, for five years, and be invisible to an archive that only records
      its own searching.</p>
    <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:20px 0">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>So: before any list of documents to acquire, list the documents
        already held.</strong> The family&rsquo;s own papers are a repository like any other, and they
        are the only one that charges nothing and is open on a Sunday.</p>
    </div>
    <p>What came out of that folder in twenty minutes is on
      <a href={u("/family-papers/")}>The papers in the house</a>: a marriage act, a religion, a trade
      nine years earlier than anything else held, a birthplace that closed the very question the trip
      was to answer, and a blank line on a consular form that says more about the Rosario search than
      three weeks of reading registers did.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Search for the people standing next to the person</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This family is written <em>Lerena, Llerena, Lorena, Serena, Lerina, Sancio, Gerena</em> and
      <em>Levena</em> across the registers that hold it. <strong>A surname box cannot reach it
      reliably.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The parents can.</strong> Searching for a couple &mdash; father <em>Sancho</em>, mother
      <em>Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez</em> &mdash; rather than for their surname returned four of
      Sancho&rsquo;s children in one query, including two the archive had never met. Godparents, spouses
      and witnesses work the same way: <strong>their names are spelled ordinarily, and they are standing
      in the same act.</strong></p>
    <p class="cap">Turned on the central question the same hour, the method found nothing &mdash; which
      is itself the strongest form the central null has yet taken.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
<h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Corrections are the most valuable thing anyone can send. A family member with a date, a photograph of a
    document, or a flat contradiction of something written here will change the archive, and the change will
    be recorded rather than quietly made.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Two kinds of mark, and why both exist</h2>
  <p class="dek">A reader meeting a coloured chip should know which question it answers. There are two,
    and they are not the same question.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">How well is it known?</div>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">
        <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span>
        <span class="chip lore">Family lore</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Used on the narrative pages, against a <em>claim</em>. This is
        the vocabulary the Defranceski and Falco archives use too.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">Is this person one of ours?</div>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">
        <span class="chip doc">Connected</span> <span class="chip">Excluded</span>
        <span class="chip inf">Unplaced</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Used in the register and on person pages, against a
        <em>person</em>. Most of the register is unplaced, and that is the point of the page.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">A person can be <strong>documented</strong> and
    <strong>unplaced</strong> at once &mdash; well recorded, and not ours. Keeping the two marks separate
    is what stops a good record turning into a relative.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Living people appear by name only &mdash; no dates, no places, no photographs.</strong>
      That is the rule shared across this family&rsquo;s archives, and it is stricter in practice than it
      sounds: a birth date and a city together identify a person completely.</p>
    <p>Where a living person&rsquo;s evidence bears on a dead one &mdash; a DNA test, a death notice they
      signed &mdash; <strong>the inference is published and the person is not</strong>.
      <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA page &rarr;</a> is the worked example.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>This rule was broken until 10 September 2026</strong>, when the direct-line page
      was found to be publishing a living person&rsquo;s exact birth date, two cities and both marriages
      beneath a caption claiming the details were withheld. Recorded on
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-dgs-audit">Every film number, tested</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive cites <strong>58 FamilySearch film numbers</strong>. Two Montevideo attributions
    had already failed inspection, which put every catalogue-derived number under suspicion. All 58
    were tested on 20 September 2026. <strong>Fifty-seven resolve to a real film and not one points at
    a film that does not exist.</strong></p>
    <p>It was done with <strong>FamilySearch signed out</strong>, because the thumbnail endpoint is
    public. Two traps are recorded below, and both would have produced false corrections against
    perfectly good data &mdash; one of them caught me.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:15rem">Finding</th><th style="width:20rem">Detail</th><th>What it means</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {dgs.map((r) => { const c = Object.values(r); return (
          <tr>
            <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
          </tr>); })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-forename-traps">Two opposite forename traps</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The two free sources this archive leans on hardest behave in <strong>opposite ways</strong>, and
    each punishes the habit the other rewards. Getting them the wrong way round produces a false null
    on either site, and a false null is the most expensive mistake this archive can make &mdash; it
    closes a door that was never shut.</p>
    <p>Both were proved here with controls. <strong>On FamilySearch, an exact forename search matches
    the whole given-name string</strong>: exact <em>Maria</em> returns 53 Uruguayan Lerenas and
    <strong>Maria Lili Lerena is not one of them</strong>, while exact <em>Maria Lili</em> returns her
    alone. <strong>On FreeBMD the reverse holds</strong> &mdash; the printed index abbreviates the
    second forename, so <em>Horace Archibald</em> finds nothing while <em>Horace A</em> is sitting
    there.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:13rem">Source</th><th style="width:16rem">The trap</th>
        <th style="width:18rem">How it was proved</th><th>What to do</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {traps.map((r) => { const c = Object.values(r); return (
          <tr>
            <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
          </tr>); })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
